"Preface - The Pleasures of Counting (https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/pleasures-of-counting/preface/AF99AD9A25450161B8FBAB44CD156B53)\nciteturn587search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; I remember with particular pleasure from my own childhood the book From Simple Numbers to the Calculus by Colerus with its uncompromising opening: ... Körner, University of Cambridge  * Book: The Pleasures of Counting\n\n# Preface\n\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  05 May 2014\n\nT. W. Körner\n\nShow author details\n\n* * *\n\nT. W. Körner\n    Affiliation:\n\nUniversity of Cambridge\n\n* * *\n\nBook contents\n\n  * Frontmatter\n  * Contents\n  * Preface\n  * I The uses of abstraction\n  * II Meditations on measurement\n  * III The pleasures of computation\n  * IV Enigma variations\n  * V The pleasures of thought\n  * Appendix 1 Further reading\n  * Appendix 2 Some notations\n  * Appendix 3 Sources\n  * Bibliography\n  * Index\n  * Acknowledgements\n\nGet access\n\n* * *\n\n## Summary\n\nThis book is meant, first of all, for able school children of 14 and over and first year undergraduates who are interested in mathematics and would like to learn something of what it looks like at a higher level. There exist several books with a similar aim. I remember with particular pleasure from my own childhood the book From Simple Numbers to the Calculus by Colerus with its uncompromising opening:\n\nMathematics is a trap. If you are once caught in this trap you hardly ever get out again to find your way back to the original state of mind in which you were before you began to investigate mathematics.\n\nIn Appendix Al.l I list and discuss some of them. However, the aim is so worthwhile and the number of such books so limited that I feel no hesitation in adding one more.\n\nIn many American universities there are courses known universally, if not officially, as ‘Maths for poets’. This book does not belong to that genre. It is intended, rather, as ‘Maths for mathematicians’ — for mathematicians who know very little mathematics as yet, but who, perhaps, will one day give lectures which the present author attends open-mouthed with admiration†.\n\nI hope that this book will also be enjoyed by my fellow professionals and by those general readers who value mathematics without fearing it‡.\n\n* * *\n\n## Information\n\nType\n    Chapter\n\nInformation\n    \n\nThe Pleasures of Counting , pp. viii - x\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107050563.001 [Opens in a new window]\n\nPublisher: Cambridge University Press\n\nPrint publication year: 1996\n\n* * *\n\n  * Preface\n  * T. W. Körner, University of Cambridge\n  * Book: The Pleasures of Counting\n  * Online publication: 05 May 2014\n  * Chapter DOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107050563.001\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContents - The Pleasures of Counting (https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/pleasures-of-counting/contents/C4FE6144C674C86262AE7995A0B9E111)\nciteturn587search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Körner ... University of CambridgeBook contents ... The Pleasures of Counting , pp. v - vii\n\n# Contents\n\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  05 May 2014\n\nT. W. Körner\n\nShow author details\n\n* * *\n\nT. W. Körner\n    Affiliation:\n\nUniversity of Cambridge\n\n\nBook contents\n\n  * Frontmatter\n  * Contents\n  * Preface\n  * I The uses of abstraction\n  * II Meditations on measurement\n  * III The pleasures of computation\n  * IV Enigma variations\n  * V The pleasures of thought\n  * Appendix 1 Further reading\n  * Appendix 2 Some notations\n  * Appendix 3 Sources\n  * Bibliography\n  * Index\n  * Acknowledgements\n\nGet access\n\n* * *\n\n## Summary\n\nA summary is not available for this content so a preview has been provided. Please use the Get access link above for information on how to access this content.\n\nImage: Image of the 